        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 没事",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "没事 — méishì — \"it's nothing; to be all right\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "没事我很好 méishì wǒ hěn hǎo — It's nothing. I'm fine."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 事 (shì) — eight strokes: a horizontal, a small 口, a horizontal that turns down, a long horizontal, a shorter one, and last a vertical with a hook down through them all. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "没事 (méishì) is built from 没 'not to have' and 事 (shì) 'matter', both inherited from Middle Chinese, mwot and dzriH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription). The dictionary glosses the first 'to sink' and the second 'to employ' and 'to set up'; 'a matter' is the everyday sense, not the gloss. Mwot would regularly give mò, not méi."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
